WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.690
In late May of 2020,

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:06.150
when the death of George Floyd

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.830
sparked outrage across the United States,

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.600
it seemed like every major
corporation wanted to

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.920
"join the conversation",
including Apple, Twitter,

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.020
Sony, Amazon, Nike, Spotify,
YouTube, Facebook, Verizon,

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.120
Adobe, Zoom, T-Mobile, Alphabet, Nintendo.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.423
You get the idea.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.070
At one moment, all of corporate America

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.560
was trying to respond to this
moment of anti-black racism,

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.570
but there was one
company that really just,

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.994
their statement just
stood out among the pack.

00:00:33.994 --> 00:00:35.293
And that was Ben &amp; Jerry's.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.700
It wasn't just nice platitudes.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.053
It was actually legislation
that they were calling for.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.440
But it's interesting if you go on

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.480
Ben and Jerry's Instagram
page or Twitter account

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.970
they're constantly
talking about these issues

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.930
and it's not just when they're prompted.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.100
It's part of their actual mission.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.337
What's going on is genocide.

00:00:58.337 --> 00:00:59.220
And that mission

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.910
has been the cornerstone of
the brand for over 30 years.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.499
But I will tell you-

00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:07.520
Bernie, Bernie, Bernie.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:08.530
But does Ben &amp; Jerry's,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.860
which in the year 2000 became a subsidiary

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.260
of a multinational corporate conglomerate

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.700
really care about social justice

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.823
or is it all just a clever
way to peddle more pints?

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.433
Hi, I'm Ben.

00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:27.266
I'm Jerry.

00:01:27.266 --> 00:01:28.880
You know, we may not have
the money to go on TV

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:29.713
for 30 seconds,

00:01:29.713 --> 00:01:31.240
but we sure do make some
of the best ice cream

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.073
you ever tasted.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.066
Look for us on top of every pint.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.980
In 1978 when lifelong friends,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.150
Ben Cohen and Jerry Greenfield,

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.490
opened their first ice cream
shop in Burlington, Vermont,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.000
the brand wasn't focused
on activism per se.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.710
We never called it a social mission,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.420
but the business was values driven

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.810
from the earliest days.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.700
I don't know that we were
hardcore hippies per se,

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.180
but we certainly believe
in peace and love.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.980
So when we started as a
little homemade ice cream shop

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.810
our idea was to be a little corner store

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.230
that would be a community gathering place.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.200
They would donate free ice cream

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.210
to nonprofit organizations,

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:18.730
people who are using the ice cream

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:22.720
to have celebrations or
for fundraising events.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.243
The policy was not to turn down anybody.

00:02:26.591 --> 00:02:29.150
They were basically
social justice activists

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.740
who had stumbled into making ice cream

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.260
and found that they had
a lot of success at it.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.180
And as the years went on,

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.080
they became more and
more skilled and excited

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.363
to push various kinds of social causes.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.830
Our feeling at the time
was that essentially

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.570
business is a machine for making money,

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.800
and that if we want to
be of as much service

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.760
to the community as possible,

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:57.790
what we need to do

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:00.830
is give away as much
our profits as possible.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.693
And so we started The
Ben &amp; Jerry's Foundation.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:06.210
The Ben &amp; Jerry's Foundation

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.380
would fund community organizations
and local initiatives,

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:12.840
helping disadvantaged
groups across the country.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.950
And the foundation was to receive

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:18.493
seven and a half percent of
the company's pre-tax profits.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.930
Which was the highest percentage

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:22.750
of any publicly held company.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.520
The corporate average is
around one and a half percent.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:26.930
I said 5%.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.370
Ben said 10%, and we
settled on seven and a half.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:31.980
You know, the investment
bankers, you know,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.240
they were in disbelief,

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.130
because even 5% to them made no sense.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.650
And soon the founders

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.600
began integrating their
activism into products

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:41.840
like Peace Pops,

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.440
a popsicle that would fund
a new anti-war charity,

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.980
an idea which in itself
caused some conflict.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.490
There was always a tension
on the board of directors

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:55.020
between those who wanted to really commit

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.700
to pursuing social justice objectives.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.240
And those who said no,
really what we got to do

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.460
first of all, is make sure that
we do a solid financial job

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.780
of selling ice cream,
that we have good profits.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.560
But over time, the guys and the business

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.440
tried to figure out how to incorporate

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.190
the values of the founders

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.810
more into the day to
day business decisions.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:23.810
And that was a real evolution
over a long period of time,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.020
with some arguing back and
forth and some struggles,

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:30.970
but eventually led to the
creation of a mission statement.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.670
That's based on this concept

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.693
that was called Linked Prosperity.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.880
The idea behind Linked Prosperity

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.200
is that as the company prospers,

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.600
everything and everyone
it touches will prosper.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.850
The company would have three goals

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:48.260
and the goals were equally important

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.480
and making a profit was
only one of the goals.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.960
The second goal was
making the best quality

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.390
ice cream possible.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:59.300
And the third goal was making
a significant commitment

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:04.070
to social justice and the
prosperity of the communities

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:05.493
that Ben &amp; Jerry's served.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.300
But the Linked Prosperity concept

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:10.783
wasn't entirely new.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.930
The executives of businesses
in the '50s and '60s

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.930
were considered members of the community

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:22.290
and their businesses
were expected to support

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:23.660
their communities.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.590
And they were looked down upon

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.410
if they did not participate as leaders

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.430
in the lives of their communities.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.707
High up in a tower in Midtown Manhattan

00:05:33.707 --> 00:05:36.201
are the offices of an unique organization,

00:05:36.201 --> 00:05:37.810
devoted primarily not to making money,

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:39.880
but to giving it away wisely.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.560
This started to change
really in the late 1960s,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.060
early 1970s.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.360
The change in attitude
was highly identified

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.230
with an economist named Milton Friedman.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.910
I'm pleased to present the number one

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:54.180
selling book in America, "Free To Choose."

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.780
Please welcome the Nobel
Laureate in Economics,

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:57.873
Milton Friedman.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.410
And Milton Friedman's position

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.563
was that the purpose of
business is to make profits.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.540
If you do anything else besides making

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.320
as much profit as possible
with your business,

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:10.630
you're doing it wrong.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.480
Just tell me where in the world you find

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:17.170
these angels who are going
to organize society for us?

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:19.170
Well, I don't even trust you to do that.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.750
I studied that, you know,
in college and as an MBA,

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:24.850
I didn't believe it then

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:26.710
and I certainly don't believe it now.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:30.350
I never envisioned the
integration of a social mission

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.080
into a business the way that Ben did.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:36.650
I mean, I think he was really
visionary, but in my mind,

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.900
and certainly in Ben's mind,

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:43.600
there's no way for a business
to really be truly successful

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.850
if it's just focused on making money,

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:50.470
it needs to be mindful of
its other constituencies,

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:52.960
its employees, its immediate community,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.943
the broader community, even the world.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.740
And I think Ben &amp; Jerry's
played an integral role

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:03.163
in hopefully putting to rest
Milton Friedman's famous quote.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:05.530
Cementing the company's priority

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.160
has allowed Ben &amp; Jerry's to integrate

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.190
more social activism into the company's

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:12.750
day to day operation, like
sourcing fair trade ingredients

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.960
even if it meant paying higher premiums,

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.480
partnering with socially
responsible suppliers,

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:18.970
like a bakery in Yonkers,

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:21.120
providing jobs to formerly incarcerated

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:22.650
and low-income residents,

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:25.890
campaigning against artificial
growth hormones for cows,

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:29.740
supporting LGBTQ rights,
promoting environmental justice

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:30.573
and more.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.910
It was really about fairness and justice

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:38.910
and standing up for people whose
voices are often not heard.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:46.050
And that's pretty different
from a corporate approach.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.880
But I think making ice cream
allows us to talk with people

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.610
and connect with people
about serious things

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:55.073
in a pretty fun way.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:00.180
As you give, you receive,
as you help others,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.513
you're helped in return,

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:05.460
as your business helps the community,

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.500
the community supports your business.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.580
Now that generated, you know,

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.800
a lot of favorable press,

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.950
which we think contributes
to people wanting to support

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:20.300
and buy Ben &amp; Jerry's ice cream,

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.930
but it didn't originate
from people sitting

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:25.020
around in the marketing department saying,

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:28.563
hey you know, how can we
generate some more press?

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:31.630
Still over the years,

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:35.120
the values triad, product,
profit, and community,

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.710
proved to be a difficult
balance to maintain,

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.670
especially with increasing pressure

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:42.830
to compete with other super
premium ice cream brands.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.610
As the company grew larger and larger

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:49.260
and the profit margins
grew smaller and smaller,

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.630
more of the shareholders of Ben &amp; Jerry's

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:55.420
began to question the company's commitment

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:58.880
to spending extra money on
things like employee benefits

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.960
and charitable gifts and special
arrangements with suppliers

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.510
in tropical countries.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.093
As a publicly held company,

00:09:07.082 --> 00:09:10.690
you know, if your stock
price is not moving up

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:14.270
and you become an attractive takeover,

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.170
you're susceptible to that.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.490
There were a number of
businesses that, you know,

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:20.737
expressed an interest in the company,

00:09:20.737 --> 00:09:23.370
Ben and Jerry and a number
of people on the board

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:24.673
did not want to sell.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:29.340
And they fought with the
other directors for 18 months,

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:30.720
but they eventually lost,

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:33.430
because not selling would
have meant laying off

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.700
probably 40% of their staff.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.313
So they decided to sell to Unilever.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:40.833
Unilever,

00:09:40.833 --> 00:09:43.260
the multinational consumer
goods conglomerate,

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:47.863
owns Axe, Dove, Lipton,
and about 400 other brands.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:51.990
The board was able to
negotiate a very unique deal

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:56.490
in which a unique Ben &amp;
Jerry's board would remain

00:09:56.490 --> 00:10:01.490
and they would have a say
in who the CEO that Unilever

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:04.160
put in place was,

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:07.500
and Unilever committed
to continue to donate

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.543
seven and a half percent to
The Ben &amp; Jerry's Foundation.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.970
And then there were some very dark years

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:16.110
when Unilever really
didn't manage Ben &amp; Jerry's

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:17.810
very intelligently

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:20.700
and Ben and Jerry's has
sort of lost its way.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.840
But about 10 years ago,

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:24.300
Unilever and Ben &amp; Jerry's

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:29.230
renegotiated their
relationship and Ben &amp; Jerry's

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:33.040
was allowed to fully recommit
to the three-part mission.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:38.040
So when that happened, Ben
&amp; Jerry's really took off.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:40.440
And I think the company is
about four times as large

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.170
in terms of sales now,

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:45.673
as it was when Unilever
purchased it in the year 2000.

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:49.293
The Tonight Dough.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.160
Some of the concepts that
Ben and Jerry spoke of

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:59.380
early on that investment bankers
you know, just, you know,

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:03.040
went white at, are now
part of the vernacular

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:07.730
in how businesses operate
today from the Fortune 500

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:09.210
on down.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:10.260
Earlier this month,

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:12.050
Ben &amp; Jerry's announced their support

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:13.910
for the Black Lives Matter Movement

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:15.430
Black Lives Matter members say

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:17.200
they question supporting businesses

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:19.960
who don't take stances against oppression.

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:21.270
There's a lot of data that shows

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:23.210
that customers are
really looking for brands

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:26.210
and companies to respond
to social activism.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:28.180
And there was one stat
that really stood out to me

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.970
that two thirds of Gen Z
really look towards the brands

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:32.870
and how they respond to Black Lives Matter

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:35.520
and the way they react
will permanently decide

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.220
how they spend with them in the future.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:43.120
I think the company was able
to speak out so strongly

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.513
and so clearly because it
had been doing the work.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:53.280
And so that led up to
the George Floyd murder

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.680
and the company being able to say

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:59.843
we must dismantle white supremacy.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.100
One of the most wonderful things

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.550
is that we had nothing to do with it.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:10.700
People probably don't realize
Ben and I work at the company,

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:12.830
but we're not involved in the management,

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:16.490
we're not involved in operations,

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:19.440
we're kind of pretty faces, you know,

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.070
we're Ben and Jerry,

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:26.070
and the company came out very
strong and it was wonderful.

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:32.330
You can imagine how proud Ben and I are

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:35.000
that the company is
doing things like that.

